  "tutorial_example_entry": {
    "lesson_index": 5,
    "lesson_title": "Fourier Coefficients of a Periodic Exponential",
    "lesson_core_move": "Compute the complex Fourier-series coefficient integral for one period of an exponentially shaped periodic signal by combining the signal exponent with the harmonic kernel to obtain each harmonic coefficient.",
    "section_count": 5,
    "sections": [
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Coefficients of a Periodic Exponential",
        "section_index": 1,
        "section_title": "Introduction to Fourier Coefficients of a Periodic Exponential",
        "step_type": "tutorial",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "tutorial_content": {
          "blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "A periodic complex exponential such as $x(t)=e^{jm\\omega_0 t}$ on $0 \\le t < T$ is already a single Fourier harmonic. In this section, the goal is to see which coefficient survives when we apply the coefficient formula over one period.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "c_k=\\frac{1}{T}\\int_0^T x(t)e^{-jk\\omega_0 t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Use the standard period relation $\\omega_0=\\frac{2\\pi}{T}$ so the coefficient is measured over exactly one cycle. That keeps the setup tied to one full period and makes the matching-harmonic test meaningful.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "\\omega_0=\\frac{2\\pi}{T}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now substitute $x(t)=e^{jm\\omega_0 t}$ into the integral. The two exponentials combine, and the exponent becomes the difference $m-k$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "x(t)=e^{jm\\omega_0 t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "c_k=\\frac{1}{T}\\int_0^T e^{j(m-k)\\omega_0 t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "That difference tells you the two outcomes to watch for later: if $m-k=0$, the matching harmonic remains; if $m-k\\ne0$, the exponential averages away over the full period. The next step will use that split to evaluate specific coefficients.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Coefficients of a Periodic Exponential",
        "section_index": 2,
        "section_title": "Setting Up the One-Period Coefficient Integral",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For the periodic exponential $x(t)=e^{j3\\omega_0 t}$ over one period $0 \\le t < T$, set up the complex Fourier-series coefficient integral for $c_2$.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The cue is a periodic complex exponential on one full period, so the right starting point is the standard one-period Fourier coefficient formula. Keep the normalization factor and the full-period limits exactly as written, with $\\omega_0=\\frac{2\\pi}{T}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "c_k=\\frac{1}{T}\\int_0^T x(t)e^{-jk\\omega_0 t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now specialize to the given signal $x(t)=e^{j3\\omega_0 t}$ and the target coefficient $c_2$. Substitute the signal and the harmonic kernel $e^{-j2\\omega_0 t}$, but do not simplify the exponent yet.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "c_2=\\frac{1}{T}\\int_0^T e^{j3\\omega_0 t}e^{-j2\\omega_0 t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "This is the complete setup: the coefficient is written over one full period, the $1/T$ factor is present, and the harmonic kernel is left intact for the next step. The only job here is to set up the integral correctly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Coefficients of a Periodic Exponential",
        "section_index": 3,
        "section_title": "Combining Exponents in the Integrand",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For the same periodic exponential $x(t)=e^{j3\\omega_0 t}$, combine the signal exponential and the harmonic kernel in the coefficient integral for $c_2$ and rewrite the integrand as one exponential.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the setup in Section 2. The cue is the product inside $c_2$: the periodic exponential $x(t)=e^{j3\\omega_0 t}$ times the harmonic kernel $e^{-j2\\omega_0 t}$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "c_2=\\frac{1}{T}\\int_0^T e^{j3\\omega_0 t}e^{-j2\\omega_0 t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Multiply the exponentials by adding exponents. Here the signal index is $m=3$ and the harmonic index is $k=2$, so the exponent becomes $j(m-k)\\omega_0 t=j(3-2)\\omega_0 t$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "e^{j3\\omega_0 t}e^{-j2\\omega_0 t}=e^{j(3-2)\\omega_0 t}=e^{j\\omega_0 t}",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The integrand is now a single complex exponential with the mismatch made explicit. That is the form the next step can integrate directly.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Coefficients of a Periodic Exponential",
        "section_index": 4,
        "section_title": "Evaluating Coefficients When the Combined Exponent Is Nonzero",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=e^{j3\\omega_0 t}$, evaluate $c_2$ by integrating the nonzero combined exponent over one period and showing that the result cancels to zero.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Start from the simplified integrand in Section 3. The cue is the single exponential $e^{j(3-2)\\omega_0 t}=e^{j\\omega_0 t}$ inside the one-period coefficient integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "c_2=\\frac{1}{T}\\int_0^T e^{j\\omega_0 t}\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Integrate the exponential directly. An antiderivative is $\\frac{1}{j\\omega_0}e^{j\\omega_0 t}$, so the coefficient is the endpoint difference divided by $T$.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "c_2=\\frac{1}{Tj\\omega_0}\\Bigl[e^{j\\omega_0 t}\\Bigr]_0^T",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Now use $\\omega_0T=2\\pi$. The upper limit gives $e^{j\\omega_0T}=e^{j2\\pi}=1$, so the endpoint terms cancel and the nonmatching coefficient is zero.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "c_2=\\frac{1}{Tj\\omega_0}(e^{j2\\pi}-1)=0",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The result is the expected full-period cancellation: a nonzero combined exponent still averages to zero over one period when the harmonic does not match the signal index.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      {
        "lesson_index": 5,
        "lesson_title": "Fourier Coefficients of a Periodic Exponential",
        "section_index": 5,
        "section_title": "Evaluating the Coefficient When the Combined Exponent Is Zero",
        "step_type": "example",
        "content_mode": "symbolic-only",
        "image_slots": [],
        "example_content": {
          "example_prompt": "For $x(t)=e^{j3\\omega_0 t}$, evaluate $c_3$ when the combined exponent is zero.",
          "explanation_blocks": [
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Set $k=3$ so the combined exponent is $j(3-3)\\omega_0 t=0$. The cue is the constant integrand $1$ inside the one-period coefficient integral.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "c_3=\\frac{1}{T}\\int_0^T 1\\,dt",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "Because the integrand is constant, the integral over one period is $T$, and the outer factor $1/T$ cancels it.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "display-math",
              "text": "",
              "latex": "c_3=\\frac{1}{T}[t]_0^T=1",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            },
            {
              "block_type": "paragraph",
              "text": "The matching harmonic survives with coefficient $1$. That is the zero-exponent case that contrasts with the previous nonmatching branch.",
              "latex": "",
              "image_slot_id": ""
            }
          ]
        }
      }
    ]
  },
